0. Preface — Why Zero-G Exists

Zero-G is designed to solve a modern, brutal truth:

Most valuable data cannot be moved, is too fragile to reformat, or is too mission-critical to risk touching.

And yet — we need an intelligence layer that can:

reason over it

integrate with it

perform in real time

repair and migrate legacy systems

unify dozens of disparate databases, file systems, protocols, and live streams

…without breaking anything.

Zero-G is the data-ingest, data-migration, and real-time intelligence runway for HoloLoom and its Applications (Apps, formerly Pattern Cards).

It uses a dual metaphor:

The Loom — structure, threads, heddles, graphs, flows

Spaceflight — astronauts, preflight, boosters, launch, orbit, EVA, mission control

These metaphors stabilize UX, dev onboarding, App interoperability, and future extensions like LightSpindle (visual generation), SolidSpindle (3D), and WorldSpindle (simulation).

1. The Zero-G Mission

Zero-G is an architecture and onboarding flow that allows:

enterprises

governments

scientific agencies

individual creators

app developers

…to safely bring their data, tools, and live streams into the Loom without migration hazards.

Zero-G supports:

Static data (files, records, archives)

Semi-static data (daily syncs, CDC pipelines)

Streaming data (weather, cameras, IoT, sensors)

Immovable data (BLM datasets, USDA, NOAA, proprietary archives)

Legacy architectures (old SQL schemas, FTP servers, on-prem hardware)

Fully isolated systems that must remain untouched but need querying

Zero-G is the “astronaut suit” for working with dangerous, distant, precious, or brittle data.

